  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Mississippi received $5.4B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal government made 78,733 awards in the state, with an average award size of $69.1K, and obligations fell 32.0% year over year. Fiscal-year history shows swings in annual obligations: FY21 was $4.9B, FY22 was $5B, FY23 was $8.7B, FY24 was $6.5B, and FY25 was $8B. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer in Mississippi, obligating $4.7B in FY2026 to date. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry by obligations in the state, receiving $3.6B. HUNTINGTON INGALLS INCORPORATED is the top vendor with $3.5B in obligations. Other notable agencies with FY2026 to date obligations include the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $237M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $143M. Other large vendors and industries concentrate around aerospace support, construction, and facilities services, with Other Support Activities for Air Transportation showing $478M and Facilities Support Services showing $132M in obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal buying in Mississippi?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $4.7B in FY2026 to date. Ship Building and Repairing accounts for $3.6B of state obligations, and HUNTINGTON INGALLS INCORPORATED alone received $3.5B. The top industries and vendors together show a heavy concentration in defense, shipbuilding, and aerospace support."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What changed year over year and where did funds go?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 32.0% year over year while total obligations in FY2026 to date stand at $5.4B. Major recipients in the current year include V2X AEROSPACE LLC at $428M and Other Support Activities for Air Transportation at $478M. Several construction and services NAICS categories also appear among the top industries, including Facilities Support Services at $132M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $127M."
      }
    ]
